From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Darren Shepherd <darren@obot.ai>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 21:06:52 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(control): a VK_PACKET carries a character, not a key
 (KD-25)

The reported bug - no shifted character could be typed into a ghostty
pane from the Windows App on Android - had a second cause, and it is the
one the reporter was actually hitting. The modifier-timing fix in the
previous commit explained the symptom completely and did not fix it.

What the client sends, measured with harness/keylog, a low-level hook
that sees injected events before any window does:

    down LSHIFT  vk=0xA0 scan=0x2A
    up   LSHIFT  vk=0xA0 scan=0x2A     <- 92 ms later, 388 ms before the key
    down PACKET  vk=0xE7 scan=0x41     <- `A`
    down PACKET  vk=0xE7 scan=0x3A     <- `:`
    down PACKET  vk=0xE7 scan=0x3F     <- `?`

An on-screen keyboard cannot press a key on the server's layout, so it
injects the character itself: VK_PACKET, with the UTF-16 code unit in
the scan code. The soft shift key is a press of its own, over before the
character arrives, and shift is not a modifier on this transport at all.

Reading that scan code as a scan code asks a different question. 0x41 is
F7's scan code, 0x3F is F5's, 0x3A is Caps Lock's - so a typed `A` sent
F7's escape sequence and `:` sent nothing, while reporting the key
handled suppressed the character event that would have carried the text.
Confirmed from the pane's own view:

    [ghostty] key down vkey=0xE7 scan=0x3A mods=0x0000 text=(none)

GhosttyPacketText makes the character the event: keycode 0 rather than a
scan code that means a function key, the code unit as UTF-8 for the
text, and every modifier marked consumed, since the client already
accounted for them when it chose the character. A character outside the
BMP arrives as two packets, so a high surrogate is held until its low
surrogate lands - cleared on a focus change, like the modifier state.

The trace that settled it ships with the fix, behind GHOSTTY_TRACE_KEYS,
because inferring this from outside produced a confident wrong answer
twice: the pane's behaviour is identical under several different events,
and only the pane can say which one arrived.

Measured with harness/rawin. Posting packets for `; : ? A a`: before,
`;` then nothing, F1, F5 and F7 escapes; after, `; : ? A a` exactly.

unitControl 74/74, with the packet translation and its surrogate pairing
covered by a pure test.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
